      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{w}+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{w}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{w}-1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{w}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{w}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{w}-1}{\\sqrt{w}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{w}-1}{(\\sqrt{w}+1)(\\sqrt{w}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{w}-1}{(\\sqrt{w})^{2}-{1}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{w}-1}{w-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
